WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:08.040
El Salvador is one of the deadliest
places on earth

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:13.580
the tiny Central American country is in
the grip of a war among rival gangs and

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.280
the police

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:20.140
Successive governments have
attempted to control the gangs using force

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:23.180
cracking down in the streets and
filling the prison's

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:28.060
Last year more than 10 people were
murdered every day

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:33.020
Gangsters tattoo their allegiance on
their skin

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.540
membership is for life

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:40.380
In El Salvador they say the only way to leave
the gang is in a body bag

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:45.180
those who attempt to leave face social stigma

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:46.980
and the threat of execution

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.460
from other gang members or the police

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:53.000
Rehabilitation programs are practically non-existent

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:58.640
but in the capital city, San Salvador, a
religious group is trying to help the

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:01.340
gangsters swap the gang for God

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:07.360
"If God
could change someone like me ...

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:15.020
someone that has done so many wrong things, why
not change all these other men"

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.900
When Wilfredo Gomez was growing up

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:28.220
El Salvador was in the midst of a bloody civil war

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:35.520
that left one in sixty
Salvadorians dead and one in four displaced

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:47.500
In 1987 Gomez, like hundreds of thousands
of others, fled to the United States

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:51.840
On the streets of Los Angeles he found a
home with the gangs

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.580
"Once I got into the hood...

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:59.220
I decided to join the gang and I
had to find a way to survive to make

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:02.300
money to eat, so I used to sell heroin"

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:06.600
Gomez joined 18th Street, named after the area

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.200
in LA where most of the gang members
lived

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:12.480
They fought for territory with a

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.580
rival Salvadorian gang ms-13

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:18.000
"A lot of my
friends that I grew up with and that...

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.960
joined the gang almost at the same time
they were killed during that war"

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:28.340
It wasn't long before he had a run-in with
the police and was put behind bars

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:34.120
" I got busted for selling narcotics to survive
and using that at the same time"

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:41.340
In a bid to reduce gang violence the Clinton
administration in 1996 passed the law

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:44.760
that meant immigrants convicted of
crimes with a prison sentence of more

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.020
than a year would be deported

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.480
"I remember
my mom mentioning, 'eh you need to be...

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:54.380
careful, they deporting people now...

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:56.480
be smart, stay away from trouble"

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:00.420
Thousands of gang members were sent back
to El Salvador

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.800
including many members of ms-13 and 18th Street

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:07.480
who took their brutal battle for the streets with them

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:11.400
The number of
deported soon rivaled the size of

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:14.040
El Salvador sixteen thousand strong police

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:18.580
After almost 20 years in the US

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:24.760
half of which was in prison
Gomez saw deportation as his ticket to freedom

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:27.960
"The one thing I wanted the
most was to be free...

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:32.500
"Three days later I was on the plane on my way back over here"

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.160
Just three months after he

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:43.340
arrived he was back in prison 
serving ten years for stealing a security guards

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:44.440
machine gun

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:48.320
"I remember going into prison
and they'd just had a massacre...

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:54.100
...the smell of blood was still in there
I'm talking about 150 persons in one cell...

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:57.700
...had sleep there, shower there, go to the
bathroom there"

00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:59.860
From his crowded prison cell

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:02.440
Gomez found an unlikely salvation

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:05.580
"The Christian people they asked to pray for me...

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:08.260
...so I told the guy, 'ok pray for
me and then leave'...

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.200
...Something happened...

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:13.660
...It's like whine like a little kid"

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:19.000
The prayers
had a profound effect on Gomez

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:25.060
"I had spent nearly 16 years of my life in
prison and all along I knew that...

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:27.440
...I needed help but I didn't know 
who to run to...

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:28.700
...I had no family...

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.820
...the guy that was praying
for me ask me if I wanted to receive Jesus...

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:35.620
...and I told him yes"

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:41.800
After joining the church
Gomez began to convert other gangsters

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.900
to evangelical Christianity

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:21.660
In 2016
he helped persuade the prison director

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:26.260
to establish a separate area where
religious inmates could live and pray in

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:28.800
peace away from other gang members

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:31.680
this was an attractive proposition and

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.700
hundreds of prisoners followed Gomez

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:37.420
Some were genuine converts others just

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.200
wanted better prison conditions

00:05:54.780 --> 00:06:00.980
"I have never seen so many guys being
impacted by the gospel...

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:05.660
... guys all tatted from their face, 
singing praises to the Lord...

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:08.640
...when I first joined the church it...

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:12.700
was about a hundred men in there
and little by little that 100 was multiplying

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:19.460
...now it's over nine hundred
It's something happening, I mean, gang members...

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:20.860
...don't change just like that"

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:27.600
In early 2017 Gomez was given parole in San Salvador

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:33.000
he feared that if he left
prison he would fall back into the gang

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:36.880
On the outside there are few prospects
for anyone linked to the gang to earn

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:39.300
money legitimately

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:44.860
"To my surprise there
was someone waiting at the door...

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:47.160
... a Christian...

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:52.460
... and they had heard about me
through a guy that had parole before me...

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:57.160
... and he had told them that I
was one of the leaders in prison with

00:06:57.160 --> 00:07:02.500
that Christian movement
so they brought me here to this church

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:07.680
...they made food for me
celebrating my freedom...

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:11.240
...that was big for me"

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:17.730
Since leaving prison Gomez has helped
other newly released gang members find

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:20.340
refuge in a tiny Church in San Salvador

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.500
The inmates who convert inside prison

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:26.840
struggle to stay away from the gang once
released

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:29.060
only seven former gangsters of

00:07:29.060 --> 00:07:31.060
church members on the outside

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:34.640
the rest find their way back to the gang

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:39.100
Those who do join the church have a tough
existence

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:42.620
They have no money
no prospects

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:44.960
and no place in society

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:49.120
"Being an ex gang member here in El Salvador...

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:56.310
...and facing society's rejection is the
biggest obstacle that I faced so far"

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:21.280
"yes I made wrong choices, I made my
mistakes but you have made yours...

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:25.870
maybe my mistakes is that I put ink on me
maybe your mistake was that you

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:27.520
cheated on your husband

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:30.460
maybe my mistake
was that once upon a time I took a life

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:35.580
either way there are mistakes 
maybe mine are bigger than yours

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:37.600
but i'm still a human

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:46.500
These men once devoted to
gangs now claim to be devoted to the church

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:49.450
In many ways the church is
similar to the gang

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:54.180
both have strict rules, a hierarchy and
demand obedience

00:08:55.460 --> 00:09:02.540
Solving El Salvador's gang problem will take more than
converting a handful of gangsters to Christianity

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:06.600
The question facing El
Salvador in the long term isn't whether

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:10.930
an individual gang member can change but
whether society will create the

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:15.740
conditions that mean they can leave the
gang for good

